# Babble (2026-11-16)
#
# The owner treated a design conversation as a babbling session: threads tried for their own sake about Spw geometry, layered information, parallel streams, loops, labels as particles, zoom, a rotated or quartered canvas, and what any of it does for art or music. Four read-only design agents then tested the babble against the readers and the hosts' source. This entry holds what held, what broke, what was measured, and what is the owner's to judge. The seed the agents started from and their four reports sit beside it.

#>journal_2026_11_16_babble
#:cache #!observation
#:layer #!pragmatics

^["entry"]{
 date: "2026-11-16"
 kind: #observation
 base: "this repo at cf41ca22; the site consumer, the canvas app and the story consumer read in place; no consumer written"
 method: "one seed file of the owner's words, measurements and babble; four agents, one lens each (symbolic physics, semantics, navigation fluency, examples for spw.quest), every cited line run through the parser, the line reader, the spoken reader and the pivot reader; each agent asked to break a babble claim; their main claims re-run here"
 limits: "nothing was driven in a browser, rendered or heard; counts cover short lines under the default dialect; a row below says checked here only where this session re-ran it, and names the report otherwise"
 parts: [~"./seed.spw", ~"./physics.spw", ~"./semantics.spw", ~"./navigation.spw", ~"./examples.spw"]
 cards: [~"../../../../.agents/plans/interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_observer_postfix", ~"../../../../.agents/plans/interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_joins", ~"../../../../.agents/plans/interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_evaluation_core", ~"../../../../.agents/plans/interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_scales"]
}

^["held"]{
 # Babble with measurable content today.
 three_counts: "No pivot changes a line's ink, its mark characters or its wall characters: 856 of 856 in the physics report, and 2,192 of 2,192 for ink on a recount here over its 569-line pool."
 commuting: "Pivots at different sites commute in 945 of 985 pairs; the exceptions are exchange, two walls on one word, and one attach beside a side move (the physics report)."
 pinch_invariant: "A dimensionless group survives a change of units: the pendulum group reads 39.47579 through any units, by construction (the physics report; not re-run here)."
 derivative: "A quantity sheet's derivative matches a finite difference to 0.99975, but exists only for numbers written with an uncertainty, and the CLI never shows it (the physics report; not re-run here)."
 semicolon: "A semicolon body is a sequence in every reader."
 zoom_doubt: "The owner's doubt about zooming between document, workspace and variable has specifics in the canvas's source: workspace to document and word to letters are already cuts, while entering a node (a page load) and leaving the file map (a rebuilt stage) sit inside a range drawn as continuous (the navigation report)."
}

^["broken"]{
 # Babble that did not survive, with what broke it.
 label_particles: "Two streams on one line need not cross: labeled pairs written in turn or nested have no crossing, and a crossing merges the streams, since the crossed words sit under both labels. Labels are not the only construct that crosses the tree: loop{ ~again @loop } holds a reference to its own name. Checked here."
 dial_opposites: "The dial's order is the listing order of the lexer's OperatorKind union (types/token.ts), and a keyword sweep found none of its six half-turn pairs stated as an opposition on any host or theory surface; the oppositions the hosts do draw sit 5 and 3 steps apart (the physics report)."
 gestures: "One gesture per kind of change is not how the canvas works: only the pull down makes one kind, eight gestures step the thread, at least nine move the camera, and nothing reads a twist. What chooses is the surface and the tempo of the hand (the navigation report)."
 separators_clock: "The comma is the chord, not lanes: the canvas draws a comma body as a ring and spw.quest says both are there at once. A plain space already has three jobs (a sentence in a body, multiplication, the term gap in a sheet). The grammar's parallel separator is || (the examples and semantics reports)."
 evaluation_is_navigation: "A pivot keeps every name; a step of reduction loses one. A yield may be a second family beside the pivots (the semantics report)."
 humps_sides: "The left hump as what a name is about and the right as its kind holds for about half of 123 code names, by a word-list heuristic (the semantics report)."
 two_speed_canon: "spw hear cannot sound a seam, so a line and its summary at two speeds have nothing to play yet. Checked here."
 rotated_frame: "Reading direction as gravity is metaphor only as stated; no model here gives it a number (the physics report)."
}

^["found"]{
 # What the readers and hosts do today. Checked here unless a report is named.
 guard: "?[false]{ yes } and ?[true]{ yes } both evaluate to true through runSpw; { a | b } gives a; a semicolon body and a comma body both give the frame's name. No form tells true from false."
 kernel: "Sequence and referring back read everywhere; choice in the parser and runtime only; binding and reference compute only in the quantity sheet; application and loop have no form that computes (the semantics report)."
 bare_mark_join: "48 of the 144 two-mark prefixes, those whose second mark is ~ ^ & @, read in the line reader as a bond with a bare mark on its left, and in all 48 the first mark sits in no fragment, so no pivot turns it. !~ba is said action potential, ba for that reason."
 postfix_walls: "A mark after walls reads whole in the line reader only after a lone scene or concept: (oath_stone)=@boof reads; home[x]= and home{x}= do not."
 trailing_said: "ba!! is said ba!! with nothing counted unread, and day{wake}! next is said day, definition, wake; action next: a trailing mark is unsaid or changes sides in speech."
 one_level: "A body is split once: day{wake; eat, nap; sleep} is said sequence of 3: wake, eat,, nap, sleep."
 names_filter: "Every pivot that changes the names a line holds is marked not read (278 of 2,192 on the recount, none marked read): the names law filters what is offered as read; it is not a law of the moves."
 side_slope: "A mark moved from before a name to after it reads in all three readers in 7 of 498 cases on the recount (6 of 140 in the report); from after to before, 19 of 19 (14 of 14)."
 walls_one_way: "No wall turn is undone by one pivot (0 of 353 on the recount): the reader offers the next pair and never the previous. On x[a]{b} two wall turns give x<a>(b) or x<b>{a} by their order."
 wall_seating: "The pivot reader turns walls concept, scene, mode, body, an order this session chose to follow the Ring; the line reader's own PAIRS table, the canvas's slot lists and spw.quest's group order all list mode, body, scene, concept."
 bond_pivots: "On =foo@(<bar>) seven of eleven pivots belong to the bond, and the canvas's two rails hold cards and controls, so neither holds them."
 hear: "spw hear writes byte-identical files for day{wake, eat, sleep} and day{wake; eat; sleep}, two clicks each; names, marks and separators are silent."
 canvas_comment: "The canvas's statement splitter cuts a line at a # that follows a space unless the next character is one of > : [ { ( @ (its spw-tree.ts). The rule was checked here; the damage to carried files (a lost #index step, five fused rows, 24 of 26 files losing #!aim tags) is the examples report's count."
 arrival: "The canvas holds one arrival line per file by a rule (its file-subject.ts) and shows it as the page heading in raw Spw; 17 of 26 files fall through to the first statement with parts of its own (the examples report's count)."
 starters_test: "spw.quest's starters test counts errors from parse().diagnostics; the current seed's parse result has no such field, so the check is always empty. Checked against the current seed, not spw.quest's pinned parser."
 parse_cost: "Parse time follows the number of parts, 0.1 to 0.2 ms each, not characters, and is the same on the current seed and spw.quest's pin (the examples report)."
 reader_cut: "The reader the canvas bundles (2026.11.07-pre.1) reads !(alarm) and (alarm)! as the same slots, and pivotsOf is in no release bundle, so prefix against postfix cannot show on the canvas before a new cut and a pin move (the examples report)."
 pinch: "Two fingers have eleven meanings across the canvas and spw.quest (the navigation report's count). The fold note arms at a pinch factor of 0.68 with no speed check (the canvas's scale-cue.ts:53, checked here)."
 ocean: "The four ocean files that fail the probe fail only because /query/ in their path selects the Spw.q dialect; two were checked here and parse clean without the path."
}

^["census"]{
 # How much of real text the line reader reads, counted here over tracked .spw files; only lines that balance on their own, so openers and closers of multi-line forms are left out.
 theory: "docs/theory, 5,254 lines: read whole 22.2%, key: value 46.6%, key = value 12.0%, other 19.1%"
 workbench: ".spw without caches, 5,136 lines: read whole 23.7%, key: value 53.1%, key = value 0.2%, other 23.0%"
 story: "the story consumer's .spw, 9,755 lines: read whole 19.3%, key: value 65.5%, other 15.2%"
 other: "other is mostly bare strings in lists, particle lines (#:layer #!semantics) and words set side by side"
 spoken: "the spoken reader already has a key rule (a word ending in a colon) and leaves nothing unread on 91 to 97% of keyed lines"
 particle_keys: "keyed lines led by a particle: 244 in theory, 563 in the workbench tree, 21 in the story consumer's"
 pivots_today: "on a keyed line the pivot reader reads the colon as part of the name: %root: 293.66 offers root:% 293.66, and none of its pivots reads back"
}

^["story_consumer"]{
 # The story consumer on 2026-11-16, read in place.
 pin: "It pins this repo at cf41ca22 and rebuilt its vendored seed with the pivot reader."
 gate: "Its build check runs every room signature through pivotsOf with its own nine-mark Ring order and fails on a read pivot that loses a name; its next pivot is the first one all three readers read. Pivot order and shape are now observable downstream."
 poetics: "In its working tree, not committed: a poetics surface reads prefix against postfix as iambic against trochaic inversion on =(oath_stone)@boof and (oath_stone)=@boof, and gives each of the thirteen Ring constructs a prosodic role and a mode."
 music: "In its working tree, not committed: each of 26 chapter ecologies gains a music facet (root, tempo, swing, mode, motif, a 16-step pattern), an octave twin, and signed edges such as =boon@chapter_01_ecology{ @chapter_14_ecology }, which reads whole here with 14 pivots, 12 read."
 checks: "All 15 live chapters' note names match their frequencies; all 26 twins are k and k plus 13; a twin shares its partner's mode a whole step lower."
 rubs: "Every motif uses only 0, 2, 4, 7, 9 and 12, the major pentatonic if read as semitones, and no code found reads a motif yet; three chapter modes differ from its construct table (for &, @ and the body); two em dashes in unquoted prose are parse errors under the default profile; marked keys such as %root: 293.66 do not pivot."
 tempo: "Tempo and root are not tuned to each other: chapter 1's 96 beats a minute raised eight octaves is 409.6 Hz, 24 cents under the sharp fourth its mode is named for, and the other chapters land anywhere, so it is a coincidence. A tempo that is the root dropped whole octaves would make a chapter's pulse its tonic."
 rhythm: "torch_march{!, ., ., ., ~, ., ., .} parses clean here and is said action ground ground ground potential ground ground ground: a rhythm in Spw's own marks instead of a pattern string."
}

^["corrections"]{
 # What this session said earlier in the day and takes back.
 walls_postfix: "The observer_postfix card's note said a mark after walls reads in all three readers. It holds only after a lone scene or concept; the card now says so."
 example_count: "spw.quest has 26 examples, not 24; the count was cut short."
 canvas_pin: "The seed's pins row is stale: the canvas's main moved from 1a4157a to d4e3979 at 10:25, taking in the edge-submenus branch, and the site consumer's version record names d4e3979."
 seed_rows: "Two measured rows in the seed are corrected by the semantics report: the dot row covers only the loose dot, and the spoken reader breaks on the mode wall, not on the crossing."
 infinity_series: "The infinity series was said to reproduce on every second note. From its definition as recalled, every fourth note gives it back and every second gives it inverted or raised a step; unverified against a source."
}

^["examples"]{
 # Ten scripts the examples report proposes for spw.quest, 33 to 159 characters, each parsing here with no error or warning.
 ids: [two_sides, both_ways, two_loops, first_touch, contact_card, walk_or_run, from_the_kitchen, within_reach, soup_phases, corner_cafe]
 where: "each with its pitch, what it asks the canvas, what its first screenshot says and the reader it needs, in the examples report's proposed section"
}

^["open"]{
 # The owner's to judge. Nothing here is decided.
 may_a_mark_be_a_side: "Is a join only between two operands, or may a bare mark stand as one side of a bond? Under the arms reading !~ba would be two marks before ba."
 wall_seating: "Which circle do the four walls turn in: concept, scene, mode, body (the Ring, the pivot reader) or mode, body, scene, concept (the line reader's table and both hosts)?"
 mark_after_any_wall: "Is a mark after a mode or a body, oven{bread}!, meant to read like a mark after a scene, (bread)!?"
 guard: "Is ?[false]{ yes } giving true a defect, or is a wonder with a mode not a guard at all?"
 key_relation: "Does the key colon have a place in the line model, with the mark on a key as the law of its value (%root measured, ~motif potential, @theme a perspective)? Is : against = a pivot?"
 particle_turn: "May a turn change one character of a particle, ~#goal: to ~.goal:, when particles are read from their parts?"
 host_order_home: "Does a host's order of names live in the line, in a mode slot, or beside it?"
 one_order: "Do mark turns, twins, modes and patterns need one primitive? On paper all four are a cycle and its subsets with a step and a rotation; against it, claim words do not sit on one line, and the dial order was a listing."
 said_and_counted: "Should what the spoken reader counts as unread be what a listener could not tell apart?"
 hash_after_space: "Which wins after a space on the canvas, a # that starts a comment or a # that marks a name?"
 comma: "If ; and || part a strip from lanes, what is the comma for?"
 bond_place: "Seven of eleven pivots belong to the bond: do two edges need a middle?"
}

^["could"]{
 # What the workbench could do next, by evidence. Not plan rows.
 trace_events: "Build no enter and exit events when nothing collects them: named() stamps both for every rule, and a host that parses on request pays for them. No owner question."
 keyed_pivots: "Let the pivot reader read keyed lines by the spoken reader's key rule, shared rather than written twice, leaving readLine as the release contract asserts; existing lines' pivots stay identical."
 walls_both_ways: "Offer the previous wall as well as the next, so a wall turn can be taken back in one move."
 name_turns: "Turn a name along a host's order as a mark turns, so a rail's stops are lines one nuance apart."
 said_counts: "Count a trailing mark as unsaid."
 hear_seams: "Give separators a sound, so a list and a sequence differ to a listener."
 cut: "A parser release that carries the pivot reader, which the canvas and spw.quest need before prefix against postfix can show there."
}
